## 5. Alternatives

### 5.1 Go API

| Option | Pro | Con |
|---|---|---|
| A. A3 only | Already built; no new surface | Typed semantics reachable only through struct actors; no `Behaviors`, `StashBuffer.unstashAll(behavior)` or `BehaviorInterceptor` shape; cannot port typed specs literally |
| B. A3 runtime + `Behavior[T]` layer implemented as an `Actor[T]` | One runtime, one mailbox, one delivery path (consistent with TYP-01 #809); typed specs port nearly one-to-one; A3 users unaffected | Larger public surface to maintain and judge; layer must not leak runtime internals; allocation per message in closures (ADR 0010:56) |
| C. Typed primary (A2) | Closest to Pekko Typed | Contradicts ADR 0010's recommendation and the shipped A3 code; large rewrite risk |

Recommended: **B**.

### 5.2 Semantic profile

| Option | Consequence |
|---|---|
| Classic only | Typed behaviors would run with Classic defaults; Typed specs that assert default Stop, `Behaviors.supervise` or signals cannot be ported faithfully |
| Two profiles over one runtime (Classic keeps ADR 0017 defaults; Typed has its own: default Stop, signals, per-behavior supervision) | Needs a new decision set; ADR 0017 explicitly defers Typed (l.89-117, 635-637); isolation rule needed so a Typed behavior's default never changes a Classic actor |
| One merged profile | Breaks accepted ADR 0017 defaults; not recommended |

Recommended: **two profiles, chosen per spawned actor, with the profile defined by the API that created it** (`actor.Spawn` is Classic; the typed constructor is Typed).

### 5.3 Module and package boundary

| Option | Pro | Con |
|---|---|---|
| Package `typed` inside `pekko-actor` | Access to unexported runtime; no `deps.allow` edit; ADR 0011 consistent | `actor` module grows; `typed` imports are not module-isolated; dependency direction only by convention |
| Separate module `pekko-actor-typed` depending on `pekko-actor` | Enforces direction at module level (`scripts/deps.allow`); keeps `actor` small; allows separate versioning | Needs a public extension surface in `pekko-actor` (timers, stash, scheduler, props, signals/hook points), which ADR 0011 owner decision 2 allows only "with a concrete benefit"; `deps.allow`, `go.work`, `go.mod` `replace`, `CLAUDE.md:28-34` edits |
| Several modules (`-routing`, `-delivery`, `-testkit`) | Isolation | Maintenance cost; premature |

Recommended: **separate module `pekko-actor-typed`, with the shared foundations (timers, scheduler, stash, event stream, props options, extensions, signals/hook points) delivered publicly in `pekko-actor` first.** The Behavior layer needs only public API (spawn, `Context[T]`, hooks, watch, ask). The cost is the public-surface work, which is useful to Classic users too. Package-in-module remains the fallback if the Owner rejects the extra public surface. Mechanical steps for a new module are in section 6.4.

### 5.4 Testkit

| Option | Consequence |
|---|---|
| Out of scope | Typed behavior specs have no `TestProbe`/`BehaviorTestKit`; each ported test re-invents probes |
| Needed subset: `TestProbe`, `BehaviorTestKit`/`TestInbox`, `ActorTestKit`, `ManualTime` equivalent | Matches what `actor-typed-tests` actually imports; logback tools excluded |
| Full port | Includes JUnit resources and logback appenders with no Go meaning |

Recommended: **needed subset, as a package of `pekko-actor-typed` (or a `testkit` package) with no dependency from production code on it.**

### 5.5 Logging and `pekko-slf4j`

`CLAUDE.md` already mandates `kit-logger` for all logging and forbids `fmt.Print*`/`log`. `slf4j` is a production dependency of `actor-typed` only as a logging bridge. Binding source: `CLAUDE.md:42-46`. Recommended: do not port `pekko-slf4j`; map `ActorContext.log`, `logMessages`, `withMdc` to `kit-logger` and its mock. This does not need an Owner decision; the Owner decision is only how MDC maps (global fields in `logger.Config`).

### 5.6 Classic↔Typed compatibility

Options: no adapters in the first delivery (recommended); explicit per-adapter ADR; full `internal/adapter` port. Because goPekko has one runtime, most upstream adapter code (`ActorAdapter`, `ActorSystemAdapter`) has no meaning. Individual conveniences (for example watching a typed ref from a Classic actor, `ClassicWatchingTypedSpec`) become explicit slices only if the Owner wants them.

## 6. Recommended design

### 6.1 Shape

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  subgraph pekko-actor
    A[actor package: Ref, Context, Spawn, supervision, watch, mailbox]
    F[shared foundations: Scheduler/timers, stash, EventStream, Props options, Extensions, signal hook points]
  end
  T[pekko-actor-typed: Behavior layer, Behaviors, supervise, adapters, receptionist, routers, topic, delivery, spawn protocol]
  K[typed testkit subset]
  P[pekko-serialization]
  T --> A
  T --> F
  K --> T
  T -.-> P
```

`pekko-actor-typed` imports only `pekko-actor` (and, for ref string forms, possibly `pekko-serialization` if the Owner allows it; this is an open edit to `scripts/deps.allow`). `pekko-actor` imports no other goPekko module (`CLAUDE.md:28-29`).

### 6.2 Consequences and observable differences

Differences that must be recorded in an ADR and in the module README, each with a test:

1. Supervision default: Classic restarts on ordinary errors; Typed stops. Profile is chosen by the spawning API.
2. `Receive` returns `error`; panics are failures. Pekko uses exceptions.
3. Replies use explicit `Ref[R]`; sender is explicit (`TellFrom`, ADR 0032), never implicit `sender()`.
4. `Terminated` is a hook/message in Classic; in Typed it is a signal delivered to `receiveSignal`.
5. No `ActorSelection` by path strings until accepted.
6. `Behavior[T]` identity equality and `same`/`unhandled` are sentinel values, not object identity.
7. Futures become `context.Context` deadlines and `(R, error)`.
8. No HOCON; options are typed Go values.
9. Local-only `Address`/`ActorPath`; no remote or cluster semantics.
10. Dead letters are a typed hub, not an `EventStream` of arbitrary classifier hierarchy (unless E-01 is accepted in full).

### 6.3 What is not claimed

A green row means that the listed validation ran for the listed scope. It never means module parity. Cluster, remoting, persistence, streams, `actor-cluster-typed`, `persistence-typed` and the durable queue implementations are out of scope.

### 6.4 Gates for any new Go (binding, from `CLAUDE.md`, `docs/quality-gates.md`, `scripts/*`)

- GitHub Actions runs only: gofmt, `go build` and AST-filtered fast unit tests (`.github/scripts/ci-fast.go:279-316`). Everything else is local: `golangci-lint` v2.14.0 with gocyclo at most 10 and no `//nolint`; at least 85% statement coverage per module (`scripts/check_coverage.sh:36-37,98-104`); vet; `go mod tidy -diff`; deps allow-list (`scripts/check_deps.sh`, not called by `ci.yml` despite `docs/ci.md:34`); license header on ported files (`.github/scripts/check-headers.sh`); `-race`, mutation and the judge at module close.
- A new module requires: `go.mod` (module path `github.com/getsyntegrity/gopekko/pekko-actor-typed`, Go and toolchain lines equal to `.go-version`, `replace` to `../pekko-actor`), one `use` entry in `go.work`, one line in `scripts/deps.allow`, a README satisfying the README contract (`CLAUDE.md:75-84`), and an update of the dependency-direction text in `CLAUDE.md:28-34`. All other gates enumerate modules from `go.work`.
- Known gate weaknesses to design around: `deps.allow` is module-granular (no package layering inside a module); coverage is per module, so a weak package can hide behind a strong one; the CI test filter silently skips tests whose file or function names match `(integration|architecture|guard|judge|benchmark|testdata|fixture|e2e|system_test|timing|stress|subprocess|realprocess|allocation_contract)` or that use `time.Sleep`/`time.After`/`testing.Short` (`ci-fast.go:36,116-120`); gocyclo applies per function literal, so large `switch` interpreters must be split.
- SQLite parity rule applies only to `pekko-persistence*` modules, not here.

## 7. Definition of 100% (proposal)

### 7.1 Classes

- **Required (R):** implemented, or adapted with a registered adaptation; validated at the level in 7.2.
- **Adapted:** observable difference from Pekko, recorded in an accepted ADR and pinned by a test that states the Pekko behavior and the Go behavior.
- **Excluded (X):** a recorded reason from a closed list: `jvm-only`, `java-api`, `replaced-by-go-stdlib`, `replaced-by-goPekko-module`, `cluster-or-remote`, `network-io`, `owner-excluded`. Each exclusion cites an ADR.
- **Owner decision (OD):** unresolved; it blocks closure until classified.

### 7.2 Validation levels (proposal; replaces the persistence-specific L0-L4 vocabulary of `docs/parity/proposal.md` for actors)

| Level | Meaning |
|---|---|
| V0 | Go unit test through the public API (`actor_test` package style), asserting every observable effect |
| V1 | V0 plus a ported or adapted upstream spec, cited by name and line, with PORT-GAPs listed |
| V2 | V1 plus a differential judge scenario passing on both Pekko and Go with the relation id and a detecting mutation |
| V3 | V2 plus `-race` and a module-close mutation campaign |

Accepted D-10 (ADR 0033 D13, Owner interpretation): V3 requires a differential scenario against Pekko whenever an applicable oracle exists. Rows that can only be validated in Go (Go-only adaptations such as C-29, S-tier relations without a Pekko oracle, `docs/judge.md:64-102`) carry documented alternative evidence and an explicit Owner approval. They are never marked V3 by default and their level is never lowered silently; a row awaiting approval is recorded as pending approval, not as V3. ADR 0033 accepts this rule; the V0 to V3 labels remain this dossier's working scale.

### 7.3 Closure of each module

"100% of `actor`" means: for the frontier of section 7.4, every inventory row is `implemented` or `adapted` (with ADR) or `excluded` (with ADR); zero rows `absent`, `divergent` (unregistered) or `undetermined`; every required row at its validation level; the gates of 6.4 and the module-close campaigns pass. "100% of `actor-typed`" is the same for the typed rows plus the testkit subset decision. Neither includes cluster, remoting or persistence-typed.

### 7.4 Frontier and declared exclusions
